A||||5||Cooter||Ogre. . . Right Again!||||14
Z||000000||Cooter||11-28-1999||04:00 PM||||After spending the day flushing 15 years of mud out of my cooling system, I put in a 180 degree thermostat instead of the 195. No more spark knock! I have driven over the hills that usually make the DuckMobile sound like a diesel, and it ran great. Thanks Ogre!||||reg||14
Z||000001||Bruce||11-28-1999||04:41 PM||||Was there anything else involved in installing the new thermostat, a new gasket, for instance? If not, where did you get the new thermostat?||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cooter||11-28-1999||06:52 PM||||The engine uses a drop in thermostat with no gasket. The hard part was getting all the air out of the system. I got the thermostat at Advance Auto Parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bruce||11-28-1999||07:21 PM||||Then, the next question: how did you get the air out before installing the new thermostat?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-28-1999||07:51 PM||||Thanks Cooter, always good to help.<P>Bruce, If your only changing the thermostat you won't get air into the system enough to worry about. Otherwise as long as you fill according to the rules in all the books it goes pretty quick.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-28-1999||08:53 PM||||That's one of the few things about a Fiero that is really simple, changing the thermostat. Unscrew the thermostat housing cap, pull out the old one, slap in the new one, and tighten the cap. You shouldn't lose much if any coolant if done while the engine is cold. Cooter had a hard time getting the air out because he completely flushed the system. Been there and done that ol' buddy, it IS a pain in the hinny.||||reg||1
